by nicolas-grekas at 2026-07-26T14:54:13Z

Reviewed, rebased onto 8.2 and reworked. Thanks @ousamabenyounes, the round-trip problem is real and the fix direction is right.

@mtarld asked whether the lookup should only consult the property when the mapping is genuinely ambiguous for the object. The `count > 1` guard added in response does not quite answer it: it tests whether the class named by the property *value* is duplicated, not whether the entry the lookup picked is. Those come apart as soon as a subclass sits alongside a doubly-mapped parent:

```php
#[DiscriminatorMap("type", [
    "invoice" => Bill::class,
    "bill" => Bill::class,
    "credit_note" => CreditNote::class,   // CreditNote extends Bill
])]
```

A `CreditNote` whose `$type` still held `"bill"` normalized to `{"type":"bill",...}` and denormalized back into `Bill`, silently dropping the subclass fields. On the base branch it correctly gives `credit_note`. The BC test added for this passes on base as well, so it could not catch it, and its two-entry map cannot reach the shape.

The logic now resolves the class first and lets the property choose only between entries mapping to that same class, so a subclass can never be reported as its parent. Int-backed enums fall out of that too: the `is_string()` check was rejecting them, so `[1 => Doc::class, 2 => Doc::class]` always returned the first entry regardless of the enum value.

Added a unit test for each of those, both red against the previous guard, plus a functional test through `Serializer` and `ObjectNormalizer`. The four existing tests were all at the `ClassDiscriminatorMapping` level, and the defect users hit runs through the normalizer, including the attribute override in `AbstractObjectNormalizer::normalize()`.

Housekeeping while amending: squashed to one commit under your name. One of the three was authored by `v <v@v.com>` rather than you, and one commit message ended with tool-generated credit lines, which we do not carry in the repository. The code comment lost its issue reference and its em-dash, and the CHANGELOG entry now also states the tie-break used when the property is unset or unknown.

Label switched to Feature to match the classification you and the mergers agreed on the issue, and the title no longer says "Fix".

